1. Use a virtual environment

Create an isolated environment with all the dependencies your project needs.
 Step-by-step:
a. Create the virtual environment

python3 -m venv venv

This will create a venv/ directory with its own Python and pip.
b. Activate the environment

    On Linux/macOS:

source venv/bin/activate

On Windows:

    venv\Scripts\activate

2. Install all your libraries

Example:

pip install requests flask numpy

3. Freeze your dependencies

This saves the list of libraries your project needs.

pip freeze > requirements.txt

4. On another computer...

You only need to do:

python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate  # or venv\Scripts\activate on Windows
pip install -r requirements.txt

Now it will install exactly the same libraries as before.
Optional: Use Docker (for even better portability)

If you want your code to work exactly the same on any system (no matter the OS, Python version, etc.), use Docker:
Dockerfile (example):

FROM python:3.10

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt

COPY . .

CMD ["python", "your_script.py"]

Then build and run:

docker build -t my-python-app .
docker run my-python-app

