Great question — and very common confusion when working with Python virtual environments!
❗️Why your copied venv/ didn't work as expected:

A Python virtual environment contains absolute paths to the original Python interpreter it was created with. If you copy the venv/ directory to a different location or computer, those paths usually break.

That’s why even if you do:

source venv/bin/activate

It activates, but:

    Installed libraries seem missing

    Or it fails to run properly

    Or Python/Pip don't work as expected

The correct way: Don't copy venv/ — recreate it

Instead of copying the whole virtual environment, do this:
1. On your original machine:

pip freeze > requirements.txt

This creates a list of all installed libraries.
2. Copy your project folder, excluding venv/, but include requirements.txt.
3. On the new machine:

python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt

Now the environment is fresh, valid for the new machine, and has all the same libraries.
Bonus tip: Add .venv to .gitignore

Never commit or copy virtual environments. Always use requirements.txt (or Pipfile, poetry.lock, etc.) to rebuild environments.

